<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Polynomials: gradient of a curve at a point (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The graph shows the curve \( y = {@fx@}, \) with the point \( P \) on the curve where \( x = {@xp@} \).</p>
[[jsxgraph width="420px" height="380px"]]
var k3 = {#k3#}, k2 = {#k2#}, k1 = {#k1#}, k0 = {#k0#};
var xp = {#xp#}, yp = {#yp#};
function f(x){ return ((k3*x + k2)*x + k1)*x + k0; }

/* Fit the vertical window to the curve on [-3, 3], but keep P comfortably inside. */
var lo = Infinity, hi = -Infinity;
for (var s = -3; s <= 3.0001; s += 0.05) { var v = f(s); lo = Math.min(lo, v); hi = Math.max(hi, v); }
lo = Math.max(lo, yp - 12); hi = Math.min(hi, yp + 12);
var pad = 0.12*(hi - lo) + 1;
var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [-3.5, hi + pad, 3.5, lo - pad],
    axis: true, showCopyright: false, showNavigation: false,
    pan: {enabled:false}, zoom: {enabled:false}
});
board.create('functiongraph', [f, -3.5, 3.5], {strokeColor:'#1565c0', strokeWidth:2, highlight:false});
board.create('point', [xp, yp], {name:'P', size:3, fixed:true, fillColor:'#d32f2f', strokeColor:'#d32f2f',
    label:{offset:[8, 10]}});
[[/jsxgraph]]
<p>(a) Find \( \dfrac{dy}{dx} \).</p>
<div>\( \dfrac{dy}{dx} = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Find the gradient of the curve at \( P \).</p>
<div>Gradient at \( P = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><strong>(a)</strong> Differentiate term by term:</p>
\( \frac{dy}{dx} = {@dfx@}. \)
<p><strong>(b)</strong> The gradient of the curve at a point is the value of \( \dfrac{dy}{dx} \) there. Substitute \( x = {@xp@} \) into the <em>derivative</em> (not into the curve):</p>
\( \left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{x={@xp@}} = {@gp@}. \)
<p>So the tangent to the curve at \( P({@xp@},\ {@yp@}) \) has gradient \( {@gp@} \). Note that \( y = {@yp@} \) at \( P \) is the <em>height</em> of the curve, which is a different thing from its <em>steepness</em>.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
